ernest shackleton's ship has more than  lived up to her name after 107 years at  the bottom of the ocean off antarctica  endurance has been found the right way  up largely intact rust and rot held at  bay by the freezing ocean in which she's  rested for so long  when the exhibition discovered it it was  like a miracle we could not believe the  state the stage the ship  the wood there are no wood eating  microorganisms no little animals that  feed on wood like there are elsewhere in  the world's oceans it's too cold  what damage there is to endurance was  largely caused by the ice in which she  became trapped crushing her hull and  toppling her masts and ending all hope  of reaching antarctica  endurance set sail for the continent  from south georgia on the 5th of  december 1914 she became trapped in pack  ice about six weeks later by november  the crew had to abandon the ship and she  sank below the ice the wreck has now  been found about four miles away from  the position originally recorded by the  ship's captain but more remarkable is  the escape that followed shackleton and  his men had no choice but to camp on the  ice for five months until finally  reaching elephant island in lifeboats  it's from there that shackleton and five  of his men made a 16-day journey to  south georgia to secure a rescue and the  lifeboats they modified to make that  journey and survives to this day donated  to shackleton's old school dullage  college in london  this is the tiny lifeboat from the deck  of endurance herself that shackleton  used to sail nearly 800 miles across the  southern ocean some of the most  notorious sea in the world waves as high  as these masts all the way to south  georgia it was a feat of navigation and  survival that allowed him to save all 27  members of his expedition not even  shackleton himself knew they were  actually going to survive this they had  to do it and they had to go out and try  it there was no hope for them being  rescued they were about a thousand miles  away from the next humans not only did  the expedition crew survive but more  than 100 photographic glass plates made  it back too  after the loss of endurance shackleton  had to secure his future as an explorer  shackleton knew that this was one way  he'd make the money back he also knew it  was part of the legacy if nothing  happened even before thinking of the  money he knew when we came back this  tells the story because my actual  expedition has failed these images and  the lifeboats of course were thought to  be the only major artifacts from  shackleton's heroic tale of survival to  have survived intact until today that is  tom clark sky news
 

